WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.540
Film Courage: Just to go back quickly [to
what we were talking about ] so you didn’t

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:08.180
enter the script into any screenplay competitions,
but did you try to take it up the Hollywood

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.180
food chain with an agent or a manager?

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.410
West Liang, Actor, Screenwriter, Director:
No.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:19.430
I know that Melissa [Center] sent the script
out right before production or maybe pre-production?

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:26.000
Just to maybe just to sort of see what the
feedback was and we had various comments about

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.300
the script and I was able to weigh what comments
helped us and what didn’t.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:43.550
I think again going back to what a real, realistic
kind of road map for this trip that we would

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.719
be taking, it was really about that.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:51.340
Because a lot of people came back with notes
about well that thing should really be happening

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.620
on page 12 not 7.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.879
“Hey, that thing there doesn’t make sense.”

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:01.670
And I think a lot of the comments like that
are well-meaning because I’ve written for

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.309
many, many years and I think I understand
all of that stuff.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:11.840
But the conversation that I had with Melissa
that I had to really negotiate was this project

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:20.710
is really about a certain location, a certain
budget, a certain kind of vision in terms

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.909
of scheduling.

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:33.340
And so we really had to be wise in terms of
what feedback we wanted to take and what feedback

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.840
we wanted to dismiss.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.680
So we didn’t take it up the Hollywood food
chain (so to speak).

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:47.289
They probably won’t be interested because
there is no car chase or no aliens or anything

00:01:47.289 --> 00:01:49.060
like that.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.770
It was really kind of a personal thing that
we wanted to embark on.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:59.389
Film Courage: Did you implement any changes
into the script though?

00:01:59.389 --> 00:02:02.430
When they said, “This is confusing here”
or maybe they didn’t say that.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:06.649
Did the two of you say “Well, if we’re
getting feedback from an outside source, maybe

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:07.920
we’re so close to it?”

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.330
West Liang: Absolutely.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:17.670
I mean Melissa and I worked on outlining this
story from the very beginning, really trying

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.209
to talk about the characters, talk about the
relationship, talk about the plots, the narrative

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.180
(all of that stuff), and themes and everything.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:32.790
And then once we had a pretty fair idea of
what story we wanted to tell, I went away

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.830
and wrote the script because I was going to
end up directing it and I felt that it was

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:42.859
really important to be intimately involved
with the nuts and bolts of it.

00:02:42.859 --> 00:02:49.290
And then once we got people kind of looking
at the script, we absolutely took in advice

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.010
and comments about it.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:58.810
If that button is not funny enough or that
is an extra scene that you really don’t

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.340
need, we absolutely did that.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:09.240
And also (like with all films), you essentially
rewrite the script in editing, you’re writing

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.349
your final draft in editing.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:16.079
And we had an amazing editor on board who
really works on bigger budgeted movies but

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:18.920
he liked the story (he believed in it).

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:28.410
And so me, Melissa and Derek during editing
really kind of tried to reshape the story

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:34.980
in some ways to match what we wanted to do
because there were still scenes that we ended

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:41.410
up not getting to even with the intention
of shooting a short script, we still missed

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.410
scenes.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:43.410
Film Courage: Why?

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.410
(Sorry to interrupt)

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.250
West Liang: Well, because of budget, because
of time, because of schedule.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.470
It’s an ensemble film so you’re working
with the schedules of so many people.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:58.790
And there’s the budget part of it which
is like running out of time (that’s going

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.829
to take an extra day).

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:09.590
So that put us in…I mean it wasn’t a horrible
situation, it ended up being even better.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:17.639
But I do think that all of the feedback that
you have to take all the way from the beginning

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:25.199
to the very end, even in editing we went through
three or four different cuts before we arrived

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:27.020
at the final cut.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:34.210
And throughout the process we had a very private
kind of screening for about ten people on

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:43.620
the big screen and we did kind of a Q&amp;A (feedback).

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.710
We gave them questionnaires and we said what
part of the movie did you enjoy?

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:53.210
What do you think about when you’re done
watching this movie?

00:04:53.210 --> 00:05:00.569
And this questions and the answers to those
questions really helped us gauge how far off

00:05:00.569 --> 00:05:01.960
we were about our movie.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.400
Film Courage: Going back to what you said
earlier, the best solution or answer usually

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.879
wins in your opinion (something like that),
do you think a lot of people are that open?

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:15.169
West Liang: I mean…no.

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:16.169
I don’t think so.

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:31.129
I think even in some ways, I missed a mark
because with art, you have to have an ego

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:33.580
in your story.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.540
I don’t think the ego should get in the
way but I do think you have to have an idea

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:44.370
of what your personal conviction is, what
your vision is.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:52.580
So I think being mindful of it is healthier
than I think a lot of people are.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:58.129
And I’d like to believe that I can always
take a good note.

00:05:58.129 --> 00:06:03.039
It doesn’t always happen and I think it’s
true for everyone who is working in this industry,

00:06:03.039 --> 00:06:10.870
you have to walk that line of what do you
personally feel like is right and what the

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.710
feedback is from different people.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:25.379
So it’s a tough thing to kind of negotiate.

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:34.740
I think you have to look at it in the context
of where the person is coming from.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:40.409
I’m coming from another project that is
very similar so I have a certain perspective

00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:46.220
and someone else is coming from something
else and so you have to measure all of that.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:52.479
Film Courage: And have a filter in terms of
what is exactly constructive criticism and

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:56.580
what is someone just B.S.ing me and what is
someone trying to squash me.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:57.580
West Liang: Right.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.530
Film Courage: And I think that’s a tough
thing because in LA or anything creative,

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:01.750
there are all of those things coming at you.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.750
West Liang: Right.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:06.830
Film Courage: And I think anybody who does
something creative has a fairly strong ego

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.569
and you can’t a be mouse either.

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:10.470
You can’t be like “Oh, okay?

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.960
Let me change that for you.”

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.800
You have to fight back a little bit.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.680
West Liang: Absolutely.

